WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.599
The upload endpoint is a resource
boundary.

2
00:00:02.679 --> 00:00:05.687
Only authorized users or services should
be allowed to upload,

3
00:00:05.767 --> 00:00:09.001
and the authorization decision should
match the endpoint purpose.

4
00:00:09.081 --> 00:00:13.053
A support portal might allow authenticated
customers to upload PDFs up to a defined

5
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:13.473
limit.

6
00:00:13.553 --> 00:00:17.185
A profile image endpoint should not accept
arbitrary archives or very large

7
00:00:17.265 --> 00:00:17.832
documents.

8
00:00:17.912 --> 00:00:21.487
An administrative import route should
check role, tenant, account state,

9
00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:23.780
and workflow state before accepting the
file.

10
00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:25.959
Review multipart handling intentionally.

11
00:00:26.039 --> 00:00:30.125
In Jakarta Servlet, @MultipartConfig-style
settings can define maximum file size,

12
00:00:30.205 --> 00:00:33.382
maximum request size, memory threshold,
and temporary location.

13
00:00:33.462 --> 00:00:34.824
In Spring MVC or Spring Boot,

14
00:00:34.904 --> 00:00:38.762
MultipartFile handling and multipart
resolver configuration control similar

15
00:00:38.842 --> 00:00:39.353
behavior.

16
00:00:39.433 --> 00:00:43.064
Relevant limits include maximum file size,
total request size, file count,

17
00:00:43.144 --> 00:00:46.549
field count, accepted field names, number
of parts, memory buffering,

18
00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:49.239
temporary storage, request timeout, and
rate limits.

19
00:00:49.319 --> 00:00:53.291
Do not accept unlimited files, unlimited
fields, deeply nested multipart bodies,

20
00:00:53.371 --> 00:00:54.846
or unbounded request streams.

21
00:00:54.926 --> 00:00:58.955
Even harmless content can become harmful
when the application accepts too much of it

22
00:00:59.035 --> 00:01:00.794
or holds it in memory without limits.

23
00:01:00.874 --> 00:01:04.449
Reverse proxies, load balancers, servlet
containers, application code,

24
00:01:04.529 --> 00:01:08.103
queues, scanners, and storage services
should agree on practical limits.

25
00:01:08.183 --> 00:01:12.098
Mismatched limits can create confusing
failures or move resource pressure to the

26
00:01:12.178 --> 00:01:13.256
least prepared layer.

27
00:01:13.336 --> 00:01:16.117
Reject unsupported upload attempts early
when possible,

28
00:01:16.197 --> 00:01:18.523
but do not rely on one check as the entire
defense.

29
00:01:18.603 --> 00:01:21.157
A request-size limit does not validate the
file type.

30
00:01:21.237 --> 00:01:23.960
Authentication does not prove the uploaded
file is safe.

31
00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.991
A clean parser result does not prove
business authorization.

32
00:01:27.071 --> 00:01:30.702
Error responses should be safe and boring:
no internal paths, stack traces,

33
00:01:30.782 --> 00:01:33.846
storage details, servlet container
internals, scanner names,

34
00:01:33.926 --> 00:01:36.820
or implementation details that are not
needed by the caller.
